<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lalitpur, Nepal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur">Lalitpur</a>, also called Patan, is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, across the Bagmati river from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>. An ancient Buddhist city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-people">Newar people</a>, it became the seat of one of the three Malla kingdoms of the valley in the 17th century and was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> of Gorkha in 1768. Its Durbar Square and monasteries are part of the Kathmandu Valley World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lalitpur-nepal.jpg" alt="Lalitpur, Nepal" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clemensmarabu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nepal_Patan_Mangal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
